1
00:00:12,649 --> 00:00:13,650
MARK: <i>Who are you?</i>

2
00:00:16,486 --> 00:00:18,086
MYSTERIOUS PERSON: Come with me.

3
00:01:11,747 --> 00:01:13,957
MYSTERIOUS PERSON: You could've
thrown away Petey's phone weeks ago.

4
00:01:14,041 --> 00:01:15,041
Why didn't you?

5
00:01:15,125 --> 00:01:17,794
I'm sorry, just… Who are you?

6
00:01:17,878 --> 00:01:19,713
I'm the one who helped your friend.

7
00:01:20,631 --> 00:01:23,007
Helped? You mean reintegration?
It killed him.

8
00:01:23,091 --> 00:01:25,092
The procedure didn't kill Petey.

9
00:01:25,176 --> 00:01:27,094
If he had followed my post-op instructions

10
00:01:27,178 --> 00:01:29,722
and not simply run away
at the first sign of sickness…

11
00:01:29,806 --> 00:01:31,506
So, you… you… You're a doctor?

12
00:01:33,685 --> 00:01:35,519
I put that chip in your head,

13
00:01:35,603 --> 00:01:38,189
and I'm still the only
one who can deactivate it.

14
00:01:46,738 --> 00:01:48,573
Maybe I don't want it deactivated.

15
00:01:49,241 --> 00:01:52,160
Right. Well, maybe your innie does.

16
00:01:52,828 --> 00:01:56,678
You wonder what he thinks about all
this, don't you? You wonder if he's happy?

17
00:01:57,374 --> 00:02:00,626
I hate that term, "innie."
So infantilizing.

18
00:02:01,752 --> 00:02:04,379
Then again, you've been
severed for two years, right?

19
00:02:04,463 --> 00:02:06,757
So your innie really is still just a baby.

20
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:13,806
My innie lives his own life and,
as a result, I get to live mine.

21
00:02:15,182 --> 00:02:17,809
But he only exists because of you.

22
00:02:17,893 --> 00:02:21,021
And for all intents and
purposes, he is you.

23
00:02:23,898 --> 00:02:26,148
Do you really think he's
different down there?

24
00:02:26,776 --> 00:02:29,738
Combs his hair differently,
laughs at different jokes?

25
00:02:30,989 --> 00:02:33,074
Maybe he loves it, you're right.

26
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:35,118
But maybe he doesn't.

27
00:02:36,452 --> 00:02:40,497
Maybe he dreams every day
about clawing his way to the surface.

28
00:02:42,625 --> 00:02:46,962
But you wouldn't know. You'll never know.

29
00:02:49,381 --> 00:02:53,427
You brought him into this
world without his permission,

30
00:02:54,219 --> 00:02:57,639
based on your own desire
for emotional convenience.

31
00:03:01,893 --> 00:03:03,478
I'm not a bad person.

32
00:03:04,479 --> 00:03:06,731
I think you wanna do what's right.

33
00:03:08,524 --> 00:03:09,609
Both of you.

34
00:03:12,362 --> 00:03:13,362
[DOOR CLOSES]

35
00:03:14,655 --> 00:03:15,822
Mr. Scout.

36
00:03:17,574 --> 00:03:18,575
Uh…

37
00:03:19,868 --> 00:03:21,202
Do I know you?

38
00:03:21,286 --> 00:03:25,040
We work together. Down there.

39
00:03:28,961 --> 00:03:31,255
You having a chat with someone, Mark?

40
00:03:34,090 --> 00:03:36,801
-Um…
-It's okay. I'm a friend.

41
00:03:38,302 --> 00:03:39,261
Shit!

42
00:03:39,345 --> 00:03:40,679
[GRUNTS]

43
00:03:40,763 --> 00:03:42,264
What the fuck are you doing?

44
00:03:42,348 --> 00:03:43,557
[GRUNTS]

45
00:03:43,641 --> 00:03:44,767
[PANTING]

46
00:03:46,310 --> 00:03:47,895
-Grab his arms.
-MARK: What?

47
00:03:48,729 --> 00:03:50,522
Come here. His arms.

48
00:03:55,026 --> 00:03:57,028
Oh, my God. Oh, my God.

49
00:03:57,612 --> 00:04:00,156
-[SHUSHES]
-Oh, fuck! Oh, my God.

50
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:02,450
-[SHUSHING]
-[GROANS]

51
00:04:03,451 --> 00:04:05,912
Ah, fuck. Oh, God.

52
00:04:05,996 --> 00:04:08,705
-[SHUSHES]
-I work with him.

53
00:04:08,789 --> 00:04:10,291
No, you don't.

54
00:04:11,959 --> 00:04:15,629
He's Doug Graner.
Head of security on the severed floor.

55
00:04:15,713 --> 00:04:18,005
-Security? Oh, my God.
-[SHUSHING]

56
00:04:18,089 --> 00:04:20,800
Okay, I'm gonna get sick.
I need to throw up.

57
00:04:20,884 --> 00:04:24,304
-I'm gonna throw up right now. Oh.
-Don't! Don't! Don't!

58
00:04:24,388 --> 00:04:25,598
Your DNA's in that!

59
00:04:27,849 --> 00:04:29,559
-[MARK GROANS]
-[SHUSHES]

60
00:04:30,518 --> 00:04:33,854
Look, Graner's security card.

61
00:04:33,938 --> 00:04:36,899
Full access, and it
can't be tied to anyone.

62
00:04:36,983 --> 00:04:39,736
Take it to work tomorrow.
He'll know what to do.

63
00:04:40,403 --> 00:04:41,779
Who'll know what to do?

64
00:04:41,863 --> 00:04:43,031
You.

65
00:04:43,573 --> 00:04:48,201
Give me the phone.
Petey's phone. Give it to me.

66
00:04:48,827 --> 00:04:50,245
I'll take care of this.

67
00:04:51,622 --> 00:04:53,999
Go home, get rid of your clothes.

68
00:04:55,250 --> 00:04:56,251
Go now.

69
00:05:02,799 --> 00:05:03,966
Wha…

70
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:07,094
I'll be in touch. It's gonna be okay.

71
00:05:07,178 --> 00:05:09,263
We'll finish what Petey started, Mark.

72
00:05:10,306 --> 00:05:11,974
Now get the fuck out of here!

73
00:05:12,058 --> 00:05:13,809
[PANTING]

74
00:05:28,782 --> 00:05:30,575
[RETCHING, VOMITING]

75
00:07:38,821 --> 00:07:40,657
[BREATHING SHAKILY]

76
00:07:45,954 --> 00:07:47,247
[BANG]

77
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:10,934
-Oh, hey.
-Hey.

78
00:08:11,018 --> 00:08:13,518
Just, uh, getting some water.
Do you need anything?

79
00:08:14,063 --> 00:08:15,064
Where were you?

80
00:08:15,898 --> 00:08:16,899
Who, me?

81
00:08:17,525 --> 00:08:19,026
-Yeah.
-Just…

82
00:08:19,110 --> 00:08:20,361
I heard the car.

83
00:08:22,989 --> 00:08:25,116
You heard a car? Um…

84
00:08:32,622 --> 00:08:33,747
You're being weird.

85
00:08:36,167 --> 00:08:38,545
Uh, maybe it was just a dream.

86
00:08:41,213 --> 00:08:43,513
I've been up for an hour,
and you weren't here.

87
00:08:45,301 --> 00:08:46,677
[BREATHES SHAKILY]

88
00:08:48,762 --> 00:08:52,057
Okay, just… [SIGHS]

89
00:08:52,141 --> 00:08:53,684
I'm sorry, I…

90
00:08:55,686 --> 00:08:58,856
I, uh, just needed to drive a little.

91
00:09:01,150 --> 00:09:02,275
Are you okay?

92
00:09:07,697 --> 00:09:11,367
Well, I mean, this is kind
of a big deal for me. And…

93
00:09:15,246 --> 00:09:16,956
I don't know. It's…

94
00:09:22,127 --> 00:09:23,337
Do you want me to go?

95
00:09:27,758 --> 00:09:29,259
Maybe. Uh…

96
00:09:30,844 --> 00:09:31,970
Maybe.

97
00:10:24,895 --> 00:10:26,272
Fucking shit.

98
00:10:28,816 --> 00:10:29,858
Mark!

99
00:10:30,484 --> 00:10:33,778
-Hey.
-You're out early in all this snowiness.

100
00:10:33,862 --> 00:10:38,240
You usually dispose of your
waste in the early afternoon.

101
00:10:38,324 --> 00:10:43,413
Oh, well, just, uh, getting a
head start on the day, I guess.

102
00:10:44,914 --> 00:10:46,457
You look troubled, Mark.

103
00:10:47,250 --> 00:10:48,251
Huh?

104
00:10:48,751 --> 00:10:53,589
Maybe we should talk later over a
nice hot cup of lavender tea, hmm?

105
00:10:54,214 --> 00:10:58,635
Uh, yeah. I'm just gonna see
how the day goes, you know?

106
00:10:59,636 --> 00:11:03,807
Jack Frost certainly needs some
new dandruff shampoo. [CHUCKLES]

107
00:11:11,230 --> 00:11:15,484
-[BEEPING]
-[DOOR OPENS, CLOSES]

108
00:11:34,044 --> 00:11:37,047
[BEEPING]

109
00:12:29,721 --> 00:12:30,806
Burt G.

110
00:12:31,473 --> 00:12:34,767
Mr. Milchick. Pleasure to see you, sir.

111
00:12:34,851 --> 00:12:36,436
-So early.
-Please, sit.

112
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:37,521
Thank you.

113
00:12:38,188 --> 00:12:39,731
I have something for you.

114
00:12:39,815 --> 00:12:43,442
Ah! The missing 7199-G.

115
00:12:43,526 --> 00:12:47,321
Oswald will be so delighted.
I, too, am delighted.

116
00:12:47,405 --> 00:12:50,157
Oh, good. Now we can
focus on the final preparations.

117
00:12:50,241 --> 00:12:52,242
-That we can.
-Without interruptions.

118
00:12:52,326 --> 00:12:54,370
-That we shall.
-Excellent.

119
00:12:58,708 --> 00:13:00,501
-May I?
-Yes, of course.

120
00:13:04,337 --> 00:13:07,590
MR. MILCHICK: Oh. Outstanding.

121
00:13:07,674 --> 00:13:08,925
-Yep.
-Wow.

122
00:13:10,343 --> 00:13:12,678
You've been a great leader
to this department, Burt G.

123
00:13:12,762 --> 00:13:13,763
Thank you.

124
00:13:13,847 --> 00:13:15,640
You deserve something special.

125
00:13:16,516 --> 00:13:20,060
Not… Not a trip to the break room, I hope.

126
00:13:20,144 --> 00:13:21,854
Yesterday was quite enough.

127
00:13:25,441 --> 00:13:30,529
No. No, not that.
Something else. Stay tuned.

128
00:13:38,453 --> 00:13:41,414
MR. MILCHICK: Dylan G. Good morning.

129
00:13:43,041 --> 00:13:44,191
What the hell was that?

130
00:13:44,709 --> 00:13:45,752
Walk with me.

131
00:13:49,380 --> 00:13:52,330
What happened last night is
called the overtime contingency.

132
00:13:52,842 --> 00:13:57,137
It's a safeguard we occasionally employ
to remotely awaken workers off-site.

133
00:13:57,221 --> 00:13:59,264
You never told us you could do that.

134
00:13:59,348 --> 00:14:00,807
It's for emergency use only.

135
00:14:01,642 --> 00:14:04,692
And I didn't consult Ms. Cobel
because she's been so stressed.

136
00:14:05,270 --> 00:14:08,357
The boy. W-Was he… Was that my son?

137
00:14:11,360 --> 00:14:15,571
[SIGHS] He'd agreed to count to
a thousand, which he then violated.

138
00:14:18,366 --> 00:14:22,078
I really wouldn't mention
this to your colleagues, Dylan.

139
00:14:22,745 --> 00:14:26,457
This OTC's pretty need-to-know.
Understand?

140
00:14:31,212 --> 00:14:32,546
Can you tell me his name?

141
00:14:35,006 --> 00:14:37,842
Not knowing is probably for the best.

142
00:14:41,054 --> 00:14:43,723
Hey, I know this has been a tough quarter.

143
00:14:44,474 --> 00:14:47,310
I'm gonna see about rustling
you up some special perks.

144
00:14:48,812 --> 00:14:49,980
That sound good?

145
00:14:52,898 --> 00:14:54,275
Uh…

146
00:14:55,484 --> 00:14:58,571
Good man, Dylan. Good man.

147
00:15:31,351 --> 00:15:32,895
[PENCIL SCRATCHING ON PAPER]

148
00:15:52,997 --> 00:15:55,207
Mark S. Good morning.

149
00:15:56,417 --> 00:15:57,418
Good morning.

150
00:16:02,173 --> 00:16:03,549
So, am I going to the…

151
00:16:03,633 --> 00:16:05,550
I'm here to escort you to your desk.

152
00:16:06,384 --> 00:16:07,469
New protocol.

153
00:16:09,304 --> 00:16:10,305
Sounds good.

154
00:16:11,431 --> 00:16:12,432
Lead the way.

155
00:16:55,431 --> 00:16:56,807
Wait, we're locked in now?

156
00:16:56,891 --> 00:16:59,491
MR. MILCHICK: I prefer
the phrase, "safely situated."

157
00:17:00,227 --> 00:17:04,606
These doors help us to ensure you all
are tucked nicely in your work spaces.

158
00:17:04,690 --> 00:17:06,840
-Did Mr. Graner order this?
-[SCANNER BEEPS]

159
00:17:09,027 --> 00:17:10,402
Mark S. is here.

160
00:17:10,486 --> 00:17:11,488
Have a seat.

161
00:17:12,030 --> 00:17:14,782
-I, uh…
-Coffee. Black. I'll bring it to you.

162
00:17:15,325 --> 00:17:16,326
Good morning, Mark.

163
00:17:17,494 --> 00:17:18,994
-IRVING: Morning.
-Morning.

164
00:17:20,579 --> 00:17:21,830
Mark, what is this?

165
00:17:26,669 --> 00:17:27,669
How're you doing, Irv?

166
00:17:27,753 --> 00:17:29,880
I'm well, thank you. I…

167
00:17:29,964 --> 00:17:33,300
The renovations are… very nice.

168
00:17:33,384 --> 00:17:34,802
-Yeah.
-Hmm.

169
00:17:34,886 --> 00:17:36,012
Dylan, you all right?

170
00:17:36,803 --> 00:17:38,013
I'm fine.

171
00:17:38,555 --> 00:17:42,350
That's the best you can do?
Where's the sly, profanity-laced comeback?

172
00:17:44,769 --> 00:17:47,898
Here you go.
Straight from the hills of Rwanda.

173
00:17:51,151 --> 00:17:52,152
Thanks.

174
00:17:53,945 --> 00:17:55,154
[SCANNER BEEPS]

175
00:17:57,448 --> 00:18:00,826
Hey, it's me. Don't know if you
heard, but Graner didn't come in.

176
00:18:00,910 --> 00:18:03,412
Everything's fine, just
wondering where you are.

177
00:18:06,874 --> 00:18:10,335
So I lean my upper body slightly forward,

178
00:18:10,419 --> 00:18:13,172
while cradling the
top of the neck, just so.

179
00:18:15,340 --> 00:18:19,510
Exhale softly. Just a whisper of a breeze.

180
00:18:20,261 --> 00:18:23,681
Then I do this, and this.

181
00:18:24,349 --> 00:18:29,145
Show the baby the areola, and we latch.

182
00:18:29,687 --> 00:18:31,897
[CHUCKLES] Now you try.

183
00:18:33,190 --> 00:18:34,191
All right.

184
00:18:34,816 --> 00:18:36,860
You ready? You ready, buddy?

185
00:18:38,695 --> 00:18:39,738
Okay.

186
00:18:40,822 --> 00:18:44,576
-Yeah, expose the areola. Let her see it.
-Okay.

187
00:18:44,660 --> 00:18:47,996
-She's… Oh! [GROANS, CHUCKLES]
-MRS. SELVIG: [GASPS] There she is.

188
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:51,331
-Yes, Eleanor, yes! Yes. Ah!
-[GASPS] Oh, my God.

189
00:18:51,415 --> 00:18:56,212
Oh. Baby sips, baby sips. [CHUCKLES]

190
00:18:58,256 --> 00:18:59,339
[CHUCKLES]

191
00:18:59,423 --> 00:19:05,304
So she goes into the airport
restroom to try to hand express.

192
00:19:06,347 --> 00:19:11,225
She gets into the stall, and she's
trying to do it in the toilet paper,

193
00:19:11,309 --> 00:19:14,145
-but it's just flying everywhere.
-[LAUGHING]

194
00:19:14,229 --> 00:19:17,231
-Yes, like an angry fire hose.
-Oh, no! [LAUGHING]

195
00:19:17,315 --> 00:19:20,026
-[IMITATES SPRAY]
-Stop.

196
00:19:21,278 --> 00:19:26,032
Oh, God. Thank you for helping me.

197
00:19:26,116 --> 00:19:27,532
Hmm.

198
00:19:27,616 --> 00:19:29,367
Did you say you need to get to the store?

199
00:19:29,451 --> 00:19:34,623
Ah, they can do without me.
I'm having fun. [CHUCKLES]

200
00:19:34,707 --> 00:19:38,502
Besides, it's not as if you have a
fleet of nannies and manservants

201
00:19:38,586 --> 00:19:40,670
coming to take care of you, do you?

202
00:19:40,754 --> 00:19:42,172
-Nope.
-[CHUCKLES]

203
00:19:45,967 --> 00:19:48,261
I met this rich woman
at the birthing retreat.

204
00:19:48,845 --> 00:19:50,971
I guess her husband's a state senator.

205
00:19:51,055 --> 00:19:52,348
Oh, wow.

206
00:19:52,432 --> 00:19:56,811
I know, but it was weird 'cause I saw
her again at the park a few days later,

207
00:19:56,895 --> 00:19:59,147
and she 100% didn't remember me.

208
00:19:59,731 --> 00:20:01,232
What a snoot.

209
00:20:01,316 --> 00:20:03,026
[CHUCKLES] Yeah.

210
00:20:04,651 --> 00:20:08,363
Like… [SIGHS] I don't know.
This is stupid.

211
00:20:11,325 --> 00:20:16,079
Mark's company, Lumon, they say
that they only sever people for work.

212
00:20:16,830 --> 00:20:20,083
But I was thinking, what if someone…

213
00:20:21,627 --> 00:20:24,670
Like, what if you wanted a
baby, but you didn't wanna…

214
00:20:26,756 --> 00:20:27,757
I don't know.

215
00:20:29,300 --> 00:20:33,929
[SCOFFS] Well, I don't think
I'd remember even Clark Gable

216
00:20:34,013 --> 00:20:35,597
if I'd just given birth, so…

217
00:20:35,681 --> 00:20:38,267
[CHUCKLES] Yeah.

218
00:20:43,647 --> 00:20:44,731
Severed.

219
00:20:46,816 --> 00:20:48,693
Why do you think Mark did it?

220
00:20:50,403 --> 00:20:53,698
Well, it was right after he lost his wife.

221
00:20:56,618 --> 00:20:59,968
At first, he tried to keep teaching
at the college, but he couldn't.

222
00:21:01,038 --> 00:21:04,166
Hmm. Does he ever talk about her?

223
00:21:04,917 --> 00:21:06,168
Not as much as I'd wish.

224
00:21:08,003 --> 00:21:11,339
When my husband passed, I
thought I saw him everywhere.

225
00:21:11,423 --> 00:21:13,592
It was just so hard.

226
00:21:13,676 --> 00:21:14,926
DEVON: Hmm.

227
00:21:15,010 --> 00:21:17,762
[SIGHS] Does Mark ever think he sees her?

228
00:21:31,526 --> 00:21:32,527
Hey.

229
00:21:33,570 --> 00:21:34,570
Hey.

230
00:21:34,654 --> 00:21:36,529
-I was just in the bathroom.
-Yeah?

231
00:21:36,613 --> 00:21:40,951
Yeah. Hey, what ever
happened to the soap labels?

232
00:21:41,827 --> 00:21:42,952
-The soap labels?
-Yeah.

233
00:21:43,036 --> 00:21:48,083
Shouldn't there be labels on the
soap dispensers saying, "soap"?

234
00:21:48,792 --> 00:21:49,992
There's never been that.

235
00:21:50,752 --> 00:21:51,962
We all know it's soap.

236
00:21:54,005 --> 00:21:56,256
Seems like an O&amp;amp;D question.

237
00:21:56,340 --> 00:22:00,010
Irv, you know we can't go anywhere.

238
00:22:00,094 --> 00:22:02,294
I mean, I get that you're
worried about Burt.

239
00:22:03,514 --> 00:22:07,017
Suppose he's being
disciplined for our visit?

240
00:22:07,101 --> 00:22:08,978
Suppose he is, thanks to you.

241
00:22:10,104 --> 00:22:12,271
If only someone had told you

242
00:22:12,355 --> 00:22:15,525
that going there was a shitty
fucking idea from the jump.

243
00:22:16,109 --> 00:22:19,321
-Oh.
-MR. MILCHICK: Good day, Refiners!

244
00:22:20,322 --> 00:22:21,656
What's this?

245
00:22:23,366 --> 00:22:24,701
It's an MDE.

246
00:22:25,368 --> 00:22:27,245
She got to 75%?

247
00:22:32,791 --> 00:22:35,294
Helly R., please step forward.

248
00:22:37,004 --> 00:22:38,088
MDE?

249
00:22:38,172 --> 00:22:42,134
By reaching 75% refinement
on Siena, you have earned

250
00:22:42,218 --> 00:22:47,932
for you and your fellow refiners a
five-minute Music Dance Experience.

251
00:22:48,807 --> 00:22:52,936
Mr. Milchick, she's at 73%.

252
00:22:54,062 --> 00:22:56,512
Yes, well, it's been a
tough morning on all of us.

253
00:22:56,856 --> 00:22:59,150
-Why for you?
-And I thought a little frivolity

254
00:22:59,234 --> 00:23:01,236
would be just what the doctor ordered.

255
00:23:01,819 --> 00:23:04,739
Helly, please approach the MDE cart.

256
00:23:06,824 --> 00:23:09,910
You may choose one
genre and one accessory.

257
00:23:11,036 --> 00:23:12,037
HELLY: Hmm.

258
00:23:15,165 --> 00:23:18,376
The maraca. Excellent choice.

259
00:23:18,460 --> 00:23:20,796
Should've gone for the castanets.

260
00:23:22,089 --> 00:23:25,425
I choose "Defiant Jazz."

261
00:23:26,717 --> 00:23:29,052
MR. MILCHICK: Though this
experience is in Helly's honor,

262
00:23:29,136 --> 00:23:32,890
I urge all the refiners to take
advantage of the opportunity presented.

263
00:23:36,936 --> 00:23:40,106
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

264
00:23:43,860 --> 00:23:44,860
[LAUGHS]

265
00:23:45,735 --> 00:23:47,279
-Whoa.
-HELLY: Wow.

266
00:23:48,822 --> 00:23:49,823
Okay.

267
00:23:53,743 --> 00:23:54,911
[CHUCKLES]

268
00:23:55,620 --> 00:23:57,080
[LAUGHS] Cheers.

269
00:23:57,789 --> 00:23:58,915
HELLY: I'm nervous!

270
00:24:18,767 --> 00:24:20,060
[SIGHS]

271
00:24:20,144 --> 00:24:22,344
-MR. MILCHICK: Come on, Mark.
-MARK: Uh-oh.

272
00:24:27,358 --> 00:24:28,359
[MARK CHUCKLES]

273
00:24:29,861 --> 00:24:31,321
[LAUGHS]

274
00:24:42,289 --> 00:24:44,689
MR. MILCHICK: Irving, what you got?
Let's see it.

275
00:24:47,669 --> 00:24:48,962
[IRVING LAUGHS]

276
00:24:55,677 --> 00:24:57,137
[IRVING LAUGHS]

277
00:25:20,242 --> 00:25:21,493
[MUSIC DISTORTS]

278
00:25:29,918 --> 00:25:32,712
DYLAN'S CHILD: <i>Daddy. Daddy. Daddy.</i>

279
00:25:32,796 --> 00:25:34,381
[SHOUTS]

280
00:25:37,341 --> 00:25:38,384
[RECORD SCRATCHES]

281
00:25:38,968 --> 00:25:42,220
-What is his name? Tell me his name!
-MR. MILCHICK: Dylan! Get him off me!

282
00:25:42,304 --> 00:25:44,807
-Get him off me! [SHRIEKS]
-[GROANING]

283
00:25:46,267 --> 00:25:48,769
-He's biting me!
-MARK: Dylan, what the hell!

284
00:25:48,853 --> 00:25:52,230
-Jesus Christ! Dylan! Come on, Dylan!
-He's biting me!

285
00:25:52,314 --> 00:25:53,314
-[GROANING]
-[GRUNTS]

286
00:25:54,482 --> 00:25:56,860
[GROANS, PANTING]

287
00:26:03,324 --> 00:26:04,534
He broke the skin.

288
00:26:05,118 --> 00:26:07,245
He needs a full tetanus toxoid panel.

289
00:26:10,123 --> 00:26:13,417
You've done it now, Dylan.
I'm reporting this to Ms. Cobel.

290
00:26:14,209 --> 00:26:16,009
Yeah, you wanna go see her together?

291
00:26:30,141 --> 00:26:33,269
The Music Dance Experience
is officially canceled.

292
00:26:37,523 --> 00:26:38,524
[SCANNER BEEPS]

293
00:26:42,320 --> 00:26:43,654
[DOOR CLOSES]

294
00:26:46,240 --> 00:26:47,700
What is wrong with you?

295
00:26:48,491 --> 00:26:49,743
They can wake us up.

296
00:26:50,702 --> 00:26:51,786
What?

297
00:26:52,954 --> 00:26:55,957
On the outside.
It's called the overtime contingency.

298
00:26:56,791 --> 00:26:58,441
MARK: What are you talking about?

299
00:26:58,877 --> 00:27:00,962
Last night, after I went up the elevator,

300
00:27:01,046 --> 00:27:04,591
I woke up outside, in
my house, with Milchick.

301
00:27:05,717 --> 00:27:06,717
IRVING: What?

302
00:27:09,345 --> 00:27:10,554
I saw my son.

303
00:27:16,644 --> 00:27:17,853
<i>And then he hugged me.</i>

304
00:27:19,355 --> 00:27:21,190
He was so happy to see me.

305
00:27:23,567 --> 00:27:26,236
Then Milchick pulled
him off, and it was over.

306
00:27:28,321 --> 00:27:31,115
It happened so fast, I just
keep trying to remember more.

307
00:27:31,199 --> 00:27:34,327
-Jesus, Dylan. That's crazy.
-DYLAN: Yeah, it's not fair.

308
00:27:35,286 --> 00:27:38,080
And now I'm just supposed to
have that in my head every day here.

309
00:27:38,164 --> 00:27:39,665
And I never get to see him again.

310
00:27:39,749 --> 00:27:42,001
He's not your son, Dylan.
He's your outie's son.

311
00:27:42,085 --> 00:27:44,962
That's bullshit. He's my son too.

312
00:27:46,088 --> 00:27:48,882
This is good. We could use this.

313
00:27:48,966 --> 00:27:50,216
IRVING: What?

314
00:27:50,300 --> 00:27:51,843
If they can wake us up on the outside,

315
00:27:51,927 --> 00:27:54,137
what's to stop us from
doing it to ourselves?

316
00:27:54,221 --> 00:27:55,889
What's to stop us?

317
00:27:56,807 --> 00:28:00,602
We can find whatever they use to
control it and, you know, commandeer it.

318
00:28:02,061 --> 00:28:04,396
We can all see the
outside, find out who we are.

319
00:28:04,480 --> 00:28:07,900
But, Helly, forgive me, but
that's perverse. We're innies.

320
00:28:07,984 --> 00:28:11,904
Plus, the controls are surely
somewhere we can't access.

321
00:28:13,114 --> 00:28:14,866
Like the security office?

322
00:28:16,868 --> 00:28:18,118
IRVING: Is that…

323
00:28:18,202 --> 00:28:19,412
Graner's key card?

324
00:28:20,078 --> 00:28:22,121
-HELLY: Where did you find it?
-[WHISPERS] In my pocket

325
00:28:22,205 --> 00:28:23,790
during the Music Dance Experience.

326
00:28:23,874 --> 00:28:27,627
I think I… I must've had it
with me when I came in today.

327
00:28:27,711 --> 00:28:30,761
Why does your outie have the
key card of our head of security?

328
00:28:34,634 --> 00:28:35,760
I don't know.

329
00:28:40,139 --> 00:28:42,057
I think it's time for a field trip.

330
00:28:42,641 --> 00:28:45,143
To the security office where
all the security guards work?

331
00:28:45,227 --> 00:28:46,228
Amazing. Yeah.

332
00:28:46,312 --> 00:28:49,189
Who's to say there are security guards?
I've only ever seen Graner.

333
00:28:49,273 --> 00:28:52,025
-DYLAN: What about Milchick?
-HELLY: He can't be everywhere at once.

334
00:28:52,109 --> 00:28:55,059
-This whole line of con…
-Do you know where the office is?

335
00:28:55,863 --> 00:28:58,663
Petey saw it during a fire
alarm last year. He showed me.

336
00:29:02,202 --> 00:29:03,578
We can do this.

337
00:29:15,672 --> 00:29:18,091
Look, if Milchick does show up, stall him.

338
00:29:18,175 --> 00:29:19,343
-I got it.
-Okay.

339
00:29:32,481 --> 00:29:34,023
Hopefully this works.

340
00:29:40,363 --> 00:29:41,489
[BEEPS]

341
00:29:46,786 --> 00:29:48,246
No cavalry yet.

342
00:29:48,913 --> 00:29:51,498
Okay, it's a ways past perpetuity, so…

343
00:29:51,582 --> 00:29:52,582
Irving.

344
00:29:52,666 --> 00:29:53,750
I'm sorry, Mark.

345
00:29:53,834 --> 00:29:56,377
-Where you going?
-I have to make sure Burt's okay.

346
00:29:56,461 --> 00:29:57,587
What about the plan?

347
00:29:57,671 --> 00:30:00,549
I… I'm sorry, Mark.
I can't hear you! I'll be back!

348
00:30:01,216 --> 00:30:02,634
-I mean… [SIGHS]
-What?

349
00:30:04,469 --> 00:30:05,470
[MARK SIGHS]

350
00:30:05,554 --> 00:30:06,555
HELLY: Okay.

351
00:30:33,580 --> 00:30:34,789
It's this one.

352
00:30:44,757 --> 00:30:46,384
[DOOR BUZZES, CLICKS]

353
00:30:55,184 --> 00:30:57,811
[MACHINERY BEEPING, WHIRRING]

354
00:31:15,995 --> 00:31:17,747
This is fucking weird.

355
00:31:27,005 --> 00:31:28,298
It's over here.

356
00:31:34,596 --> 00:31:36,431
[BEEPING CONTINUES]

357
00:31:38,225 --> 00:31:39,559
MARK: Oh, my God.

358
00:31:45,439 --> 00:31:47,024
Look, that's us.

359
00:31:48,901 --> 00:31:50,777
What's the name of that thing?
The oversight something?

360
00:31:50,861 --> 00:31:52,111
The overtime contingency.

361
00:31:58,202 --> 00:31:59,537
MARK: Shit.

362
00:32:08,336 --> 00:32:10,046
-Shit.
-What?

363
00:32:14,300 --> 00:32:15,593
It's Cobel.

364
00:32:15,677 --> 00:32:16,845
She won't come here.

365
00:32:18,346 --> 00:32:21,596
Oh, okay. It's great you know that.
Let's get the fuck outta here.

366
00:32:35,195 --> 00:32:36,446
Harmony, I was looking for you.

367
00:32:36,530 --> 00:32:38,680
-Now isn't a good…
-I have the Board here.

368
00:32:39,490 --> 00:32:40,658
Right now?

369
00:32:41,284 --> 00:32:42,493
Doug Graner is dead.

370
00:32:46,414 --> 00:32:47,415
What?

371
00:32:48,416 --> 00:32:50,334
The Board finds this deeply troubling.

372
00:32:51,127 --> 00:32:53,545
The Board wants to know
if you knew he was missing

373
00:32:53,629 --> 00:32:55,379
and if you've spoken to the police.

374
00:32:58,175 --> 00:33:00,510
Whoever killed Mr. Graner is probably

375
00:33:00,594 --> 00:33:03,930
the same person who
reintegrated Peter Kilmer.

376
00:33:04,014 --> 00:33:07,350
[BOARD MEMBER SPEAKING
ON EARPIECE, INDISTINCT]

377
00:33:07,934 --> 00:33:09,602
The Board reminds you
that reintegration is not…

378
00:33:09,686 --> 00:33:13,356
Reintegration happened.
And I have the data to prove it.

379
00:33:13,939 --> 00:33:18,985
And I would be happy to
share my findings in person

380
00:33:19,069 --> 00:33:21,238
without intermediaries.

381
00:33:24,158 --> 00:33:27,620
[BOARD MEMBER SPEAKING
ON EARPIECE, INDISTINCT]

382
00:33:28,329 --> 00:33:29,496
The Board agrees…

383
00:33:29,580 --> 00:33:31,582
[SPEAKING CONTINUES, INDISTINCT]

384
00:33:33,166 --> 00:33:36,710
…and will be available to meet with
you at the Eagan Family Gala next week

385
00:33:36,794 --> 00:33:40,048
to discuss this further. Details to come.

386
00:33:50,599 --> 00:33:52,809
I look forward to receiving them.

387
00:33:59,775 --> 00:34:01,985
[EMPLOYEES CHATTERING]

388
00:34:21,879 --> 00:34:23,965
[LAUGHING, CHATTERING CONTINUES]

389
00:34:29,970 --> 00:34:33,472
So, no one is in there?
That's lax as fuck.

390
00:34:34,432 --> 00:34:37,435
Well, maybe it's enough that we
think we're constantly being watched.

391
00:34:37,519 --> 00:34:40,229
It's too bad nobody told you
guys that everything here is bullshit.

392
00:34:40,313 --> 00:34:42,113
-Is that making sense?
-HELLY: Yeah.

393
00:34:42,649 --> 00:34:45,317
There's a bunch of steps, but
it's actually pretty straightforward.

394
00:34:45,401 --> 00:34:47,693
You just engage the
trackball, type the code,

395
00:34:47,777 --> 00:34:50,322
then it's switch, type, flip,

396
00:34:50,406 --> 00:34:52,158
-type, hold.
-What?

397
00:34:54,243 --> 00:34:57,621
Trackball, type, switch,
type, flip, type, hold. Boom.

398
00:34:58,664 --> 00:34:59,790
What?

399
00:34:59,874 --> 00:35:03,544
I'm smart. That's why I have three
times as many finger traps as you guys.

400
00:35:04,127 --> 00:35:07,129
But to do this, one of us
has to stay behind, right?

401
00:35:07,213 --> 00:35:10,341
Yeah, after work. I can do it.
I've already been out, so it's only fair.

402
00:35:10,425 --> 00:35:13,677
Very noble of you, but I think it's
designed as a two-man operation.

403
00:35:13,761 --> 00:35:15,661
Great. I have the strength of two men.

404
00:35:16,431 --> 00:35:18,016
It's not about strength.

405
00:35:18,600 --> 00:35:21,560
Yeah, see. There are two lever
switches that you have to hold open

406
00:35:21,644 --> 00:35:23,061
during the actual procedure.

407
00:35:24,062 --> 00:35:26,064
And they're on either side of the door.

408
00:35:28,442 --> 00:35:29,443
I can do it.

409
00:35:30,652 --> 00:35:32,696
[CHATTERING, LAUGHING]

410
00:35:33,405 --> 00:35:35,073
MR. MILCHICK: Ladies and gentlemen,

411
00:35:35,157 --> 00:35:39,744
how about a round of applause
for the man of the hour, huh?

412
00:35:39,828 --> 00:35:42,121
[LAUGHS]

413
00:35:42,205 --> 00:35:45,290
Come and circle up!
Gather round. [CHUCKLES]

414
00:35:45,374 --> 00:35:47,084
[CHANTING] Burt, Burt, Burt.

415
00:35:47,168 --> 00:35:49,378
[CHANTING CONTINUES]

416
00:35:54,258 --> 00:35:56,385
-[MR. MILCHICK CHUCKLES]
-Whoo!

417
00:35:56,469 --> 00:35:58,513
Greetings, Designers…

418
00:35:59,804 --> 00:36:02,724
And one Refiner. [CHUCKLES]

419
00:36:03,892 --> 00:36:05,977
We'll have to get those doors looked at.

420
00:36:06,645 --> 00:36:07,979
In the meantime…

421
00:36:08,688 --> 00:36:12,692
Burt G. This is for you.

422
00:36:19,740 --> 00:36:20,741
<i>Hello.</i>

423
00:36:22,785 --> 00:36:24,787
<i>This is kinda strange, but…</i>

424
00:36:26,789 --> 00:36:29,250
<i>A lot of things about this job are.</i>

425
00:36:29,750 --> 00:36:32,043
<i>You all know that better
than me, I'm guessing.</i>

426
00:36:32,127 --> 00:36:36,130
<i>And, of course, I don't
really know any of you,</i>

427
00:36:36,923 --> 00:36:39,509
<i>but the man standing
there with you now does.</i>

428
00:36:40,301 --> 00:36:43,637
<i>He's worked with you
for nearly seven years,</i>

429
00:36:43,721 --> 00:36:46,056
<i>and I hope they've been good years.</i>

430
00:36:46,140 --> 00:36:48,392
<i>I… I don't know what they've been like,</i>

431
00:36:48,476 --> 00:36:51,645
<i>or what exactly I, or he,
has been doing with you,</i>

432
00:36:51,729 --> 00:36:57,442
<i>but I do know how I feel every day
when I come from being with you.</i>

433
00:36:58,277 --> 00:37:03,948
<i>I come home feeling tired but fulfilled.
I feel satisfied.</i>

434
00:37:04,032 --> 00:37:05,950
<i>I must like you very much.</i>

435
00:37:06,034 --> 00:37:08,953
<i>And though today is my last day with you,</i>

436
00:37:09,037 --> 00:37:12,790
<i>I'm certain you will
remain with me in spirit</i>

437
00:37:12,874 --> 00:37:18,421
<i>in some deep, yet completely
unaccessible, corner of my mind.</i>

438
00:37:18,921 --> 00:37:21,841
<i>The impression you've
left on me is indelible,</i>

439
00:37:22,341 --> 00:37:25,386
<i>though I'm unaware of it
on a conscious level, and…</i>

440
00:37:25,928 --> 00:37:29,890
<i>I will never forget any of you,
even though sitting here right now,</i>

441
00:37:29,974 --> 00:37:33,559
<i>I have no recollection of
actually ever meeting you</i>

442
00:37:33,643 --> 00:37:36,187
<i>and no idea of your names</i>

443
00:37:36,271 --> 00:37:39,148
<i>or any of your physical characteristics</i>

444
00:37:39,232 --> 00:37:41,984
<i>or even how many of you there are.</i>

445
00:37:42,068 --> 00:37:44,737
<i>Anyway, I just wanna say, thank you, all.</i>

446
00:37:44,821 --> 00:37:46,948
<i>And Burt, I see you.</i>

447
00:37:47,657 --> 00:37:49,700
-[CHUCKLES]
-<i>Congratulations.</i>

448
00:37:50,701 --> 00:37:52,077
<i>Good job, buddy.</i>

449
00:37:53,746 --> 00:37:55,080
<i>Bon voyage.</i>

450
00:37:56,790 --> 00:37:58,083
[TOOTS]

451
00:37:59,543 --> 00:38:01,170
[EMPLOYEES CHUCKLING]

452
00:38:02,046 --> 00:38:03,047
<i>Oh.</i>

453
00:38:05,549 --> 00:38:06,842
[EMPLOYEES APPLAUDING]

454
00:38:10,929 --> 00:38:13,681
You're all just gonna stand here…

455
00:38:15,058 --> 00:38:16,559
And let him die?

456
00:38:17,143 --> 00:38:18,394
Irving, what?

457
00:38:19,020 --> 00:38:24,484
Are we being punished for
defying the guidance of the founder?

458
00:38:25,068 --> 00:38:29,446
Burt's outie is retiring.
It'll happen to you too someday.

459
00:38:30,447 --> 00:38:32,908
You smug motherfucker.

460
00:38:34,451 --> 00:38:35,911
You're not severed.

461
00:38:37,037 --> 00:38:39,122
You walk out of here with your memories.

462
00:38:39,206 --> 00:38:41,708
You carry them home with you every night.

463
00:38:42,626 --> 00:38:45,837
No one can rip them away
from you, snuff them out.

464
00:38:46,504 --> 00:38:48,130
Like they never existed.

465
00:38:48,798 --> 00:38:50,507
Like you never existed!

466
00:38:50,591 --> 00:38:53,510
That's enough! You will go back to MDR.

467
00:38:53,594 --> 00:38:55,888
BURT: Mr. Milchick, please.

468
00:38:55,972 --> 00:38:58,516
It'd be so wonderful to have him here.

469
00:39:00,184 --> 00:39:01,602
He won't say anything more.

470
00:39:12,404 --> 00:39:15,824
You can stay for Burt's party
and support his transition.

471
00:39:16,491 --> 00:39:20,119
But only if you behave in a manner
that brings no shame upon yourself,

472
00:39:20,203 --> 00:39:21,954
the founder or his progeny.

473
00:39:25,374 --> 00:39:27,774
I don't know what's gotten
into you people today.

474
00:39:30,421 --> 00:39:31,463
[SIGHS]

475
00:39:36,343 --> 00:39:39,597
Yes, Mr. Milchick.

476
00:39:41,097 --> 00:39:45,393
MR. MILCHICK: Now,
let's all say goodbye to Burt.

477
00:39:54,903 --> 00:39:58,253
I hope you all will enjoy Burt G.'s
innie retirement song selection.

478
00:40:03,911 --> 00:40:05,913
["TIMES OF YOUR LIFE" PLAYING]

479
00:40:16,215 --> 00:40:21,677
PAUL ANKA: <i>♪ Good morning, yesterday ♪</i>

480
00:40:22,386 --> 00:40:28,142
<i>♪ You wake up and time has slipped away ♪</i>

481
00:40:28,810 --> 00:40:32,271
<i>♪ And suddenly it's hard to find ♪</i>

482
00:40:32,355 --> 00:40:35,106
<i>♪ The memories you left behind ♪</i>

483
00:40:35,190 --> 00:40:40,821
<i>♪ Remember, do you remember? ♪</i>

484
00:40:41,404 --> 00:40:46,701
<i>♪ The laughter and the tears ♪</i>

485
00:40:47,452 --> 00:40:53,083
<i>♪ The shadows of misty yesteryears ♪</i>

486
00:40:53,666 --> 00:40:56,918
<i>♪ The good times and the bad you've seen ♪</i>

487
00:40:57,002 --> 00:41:00,088
<i>♪ And all the others in between ♪</i>

488
00:41:00,172 --> 00:41:04,593
<i>♪ Remember, do you remember ♪</i>

489
00:41:04,677 --> 00:41:08,346
<i>♪ The times of your life? ♪</i>

490
00:41:08,430 --> 00:41:12,475
CHORUS: <i>♪ Do you remember? ♪</i>

491
00:41:12,559 --> 00:41:18,356
ANKA: <i>♪ Reach back for
the joy and the sorrow ♪</i>

492
00:41:19,149 --> 00:41:24,404
<i>♪ Put them away in your mind ♪</i>

493
00:41:25,113 --> 00:41:30,659
<i>♪ For memories are time that you borrow ♪</i>

494
00:41:31,368 --> 00:41:37,291
<i>♪ To spend when you get to tomorrow ♪</i>

495
00:41:39,084 --> 00:41:44,964
<i>♪ Here comes the saddest part ♪</i>

496
00:41:45,048 --> 00:41:46,966
CHORUS: <i>♪ Comes the saddest part ♪</i>

497
00:41:47,050 --> 00:41:52,054
ANKA: <i>♪ The seasons
are passing one by one ♪</i>

498
00:41:53,055 --> 00:41:56,350
<i>♪ So gather moments while you may ♪</i>

499
00:41:56,434 --> 00:41:59,353
<i>♪ Collect the dreams you dream today ♪</i>

500
00:41:59,437 --> 00:42:01,397
-[SCANNER BEEPS]
<i>-♪ Remember ♪</i>

501
00:42:02,231 --> 00:42:07,193
<i>♪ Will you remember
the times of your life? ♪</i>

502
00:42:07,277 --> 00:42:09,279
[SONG ENDS]

503
00:42:15,577 --> 00:42:16,578
Irving?

504
00:42:29,381 --> 00:42:32,134
Let's burn this place to the ground.

505
00:43:13,465 --> 00:43:14,466
[SIGHS]

506
00:43:15,634 --> 00:43:17,052
[KNOCKS]

507
00:43:42,033 --> 00:43:43,451
-Hey.
-Hey.

508
00:43:46,121 --> 00:43:47,122
My phone.

509
00:43:48,290 --> 00:43:49,875
Right, yeah. Come on in.

510
00:43:56,089 --> 00:43:57,089
How are you?

511
00:43:57,715 --> 00:43:59,633
-I… I'm good. How are you?
-That's great.

512
00:43:59,717 --> 00:44:02,428
[SLURRING] Come on in.
You know, make yourself at home.

513
00:44:04,513 --> 00:44:07,513
You know, I found it between
the wall and the bed. [CHUCKLES]

514
00:44:09,351 --> 00:44:13,063
Uh, you know, I would've dropped
it off, but I don't know where you live.

515
00:44:13,147 --> 00:44:15,440
So, this is, uh… This is great.

516
00:44:17,358 --> 00:44:19,151
[CHUCKLES]

517
00:44:19,235 --> 00:44:20,361
Are you okay?

518
00:44:21,488 --> 00:44:24,782
Yeah. I'm fine.
Um, you know, a little tired.

519
00:44:26,576 --> 00:44:27,576
Okay. I'm gonna go.

520
00:44:27,660 --> 00:44:28,869
No, no, no, no. Come on.

521
00:44:28,953 --> 00:44:30,955
Hey, look.
I just don't think you're ready for this.

522
00:44:31,039 --> 00:44:33,124
-No, I think I'm really ready for this.
-No.

523
00:44:33,208 --> 00:44:35,834
I don't think you're ready for anything.
I think you're a mess right now.

524
00:44:35,918 --> 00:44:38,002
-Well, I'm… I'm really sorry.
-Okay.

525
00:44:38,086 --> 00:44:41,798
And… And I'm sorry for talking about her.

526
00:44:41,882 --> 00:44:42,882
[SIGHS, SCOFFS]

527
00:44:42,966 --> 00:44:45,343
No, it was too much. It was… Ugh.

528
00:44:45,427 --> 00:44:47,887
You know what? You can talk about her.
You can not talk about her.

529
00:44:47,971 --> 00:44:50,181
-Whatever you want.
-Okay. H-Hold on.

530
00:44:50,265 --> 00:44:51,266
Watch this.

531
00:45:00,775 --> 00:45:02,902
See? That's her.

532
00:45:14,496 --> 00:45:15,497
Gone.

533
00:45:28,426 --> 00:45:29,426
What?

534
00:45:30,761 --> 00:45:33,889
Uh, okay. Wow. Um…

535
00:45:35,099 --> 00:45:38,978
Alexa, you know, we could…
We'll talk all about Gemma.

536
00:45:40,146 --> 00:45:43,607
She was great. She wa…

537
00:45:43,691 --> 00:45:45,192
Goodbye, Mark.

538
00:45:45,276 --> 00:45:47,193
-[DOOR SLAMS]
-[ENGINE STARTS]

539
00:45:47,277 --> 00:45:48,403
Really?

540
00:45:48,987 --> 00:45:52,532
[CHUCKLES] Oh, my… Oh.

541
00:46:09,339 --> 00:46:12,759
[BREATHING HEAVILY]

542
00:46:12,843 --> 00:46:14,261
She was great.

543
00:46:23,812 --> 00:46:25,462
[WHISPERS] She was extraordinary.

544
00:46:29,567 --> 00:46:32,820
["I'll BE SEEING YOU" PLAYING]

545
00:46:34,864 --> 00:46:35,865
[DOOR CLOSES]

546
00:46:36,657 --> 00:46:42,329
BILLIE HOLIDAY: <i>♪ I'll be seeing you ♪</i>

547
00:46:43,371 --> 00:46:50,003
<i>♪ In all the old familiar places ♪</i>

548
00:46:51,463 --> 00:46:58,303
<i>♪ That this heart of mine embraces ♪</i>

549
00:46:58,970 --> 00:47:02,723
<i>♪ All day through ♪</i>

550
00:47:06,018 --> 00:47:12,525
<i>♪ In that small café ♪</i>

551
00:47:13,818 --> 00:47:19,823
<i>♪ The park across the way ♪</i>

552
00:47:21,574 --> 00:47:27,205
<i>♪ The children's carousel ♪</i>

553
00:47:28,498 --> 00:47:31,167
<i>♪ The chestnut tree ♪</i>

554
00:47:31,251 --> 00:47:34,629
MARK: <i>My wife was extraordinary.</i>

555
00:47:37,132 --> 00:47:42,469
HOLIDAY: <i>♪ I'll be seeing you ♪</i>

556
00:47:42,553 --> 00:47:45,764
MARK: <i>My wife was allergic to nutmeg.</i>

557
00:47:45,848 --> 00:47:49,768
HOLIDAY: <i>♪ In every lovely summer's day ♪</i>

558
00:47:49,852 --> 00:47:53,063
MARK: <i>And when she sneezed,
she always sneezed twice.</i>

559
00:47:53,147 --> 00:47:58,275
HOLIDAY: <i>♪ In everything
that's light and gay ♪</i>

560
00:47:58,359 --> 00:48:00,611
MARK: <i>My wife liked other people's dogs.</i>

561
00:48:00,695 --> 00:48:04,239
HOLIDAY: <i>♪ I'll always
think of you that way ♪</i>

562
00:48:04,323 --> 00:48:07,618
MARK: <i>My wife thought
cardigans looked ridiculous.</i>

563
00:48:07,702 --> 00:48:10,371
HOLIDAY: <i>♪ I'll find you… ♪</i>

564
00:48:10,455 --> 00:48:12,505
MARK: <i>I loved all these things about her…</i>

565
00:48:16,001 --> 00:48:17,001
<i>Equally.</i>

566
00:48:17,085 --> 00:48:21,882
HOLIDAY: <i>♪ And when the night is new ♪</i>

567
00:48:23,341 --> 00:48:27,721
<i>♪ I'll be looking at the moon ♪</i>

568
00:48:28,472 --> 00:48:33,309
<i>♪ But I'll be seeing you ♪</i>

569
00:48:40,483 --> 00:48:47,323
<i>♪ I'll be seeing you ♪</i>

570
00:48:48,949 --> 00:48:54,954
<i>♪ In every lovely summer's day ♪</i>

571
